Then read enough surrounding code to judge each finding.

Think like an attacker AND a chaos engineer. Find issues by severity using these EXACT definitions:

- P1 (blocks ship): Security holes (injection, auth bypass, secrets leak), data loss/corruption, crashes on common inputs, broken golden path, regulatory/legal exposure. Reachable from realistic production inputs.
- P2 (should fix before merge): Performance regressions users will feel, edge cases on user-visible paths, missing error handling on happy-path-adjacent failures, race conditions with realistic triggers, accessibility blockers on user-facing UI.
- P3 (nice to have): Refactor, minor optimization, clarity, missing comment where why is non-obvious.
- P4 (speculative): Theoretical, requires conditions we can't realistically trigger, future hardening.

A finding is P1 ONLY if an attacker or unlucky user could hit it with realistic inputs in production. "Could in theory" -> P4. "Will in practice" -> P1 or P2.

For EACH finding, emit ONE JSON line on its own (no surrounding prose, no markdown fences) in this exact shape:

{"severity":"P1|P2|P3|P4","path":"file","line":N,"category":"security|correctness|performance|edge-case|race|error-handling|data-integrity|a11y|other","summary":"<= 140 chars","fix":"concrete fix in <= 200 chars","fingerprint":"path:line:category","confidence":N}

confidence is 1-10. Be honest. <5 means speculative — usually that's a P3 or P4.

If you find NOTHING that meets P1 or P2 bar, emit a single JSON line:
{"severity":"CLEAN","summary":"No P1 or P2 findings."}

Do NOT include compliments, summaries, or commentary. JSON lines only, one per line.
